    "period": "2026年04月",
    "folder": "0416  “这对农民太不公平了”，一场关于养老金的罕见争论",
    "titleZh": "“这对农民太不公平了”，一场关于养老金的罕见争论",
    "title": "At China’s Big Political Meeting, a Rare Debate About Inequality",
    "topicEn": "Politics",
    "topicZh": "政治",
    "byline": "By Vivian Wang, The New York Times, PublishedMarch 12, 2026",
    "paragraphs": [
      "When the Chinese government gathered this month for its biggest political meeting of the year, it laid out big ambitions. It will keep boosting military spending. It will work to dominate artificial intelligence. It will reinforce its manufacturing prowess. But when it comes to some of the country's most vulnerable citizens, the plan is decidedly less lofty. The government said it would increase the minimum basic pension for rural residents by 20 yuan a month, or less than $3, to a grand total of about $24 a month.",
      "The promised increase is so meager that it has prompted widespread calls for more—even from representatives to China's rubber-stamp legislature who usually spend their annual meeting praising Beijing's plans. \"This is just too unfair to farmers,\" Guo Fenglian, a representative and village leader who was once praised by Mao as a model worker, told a Chinese reporter at the weeklong legislative meeting in Beijing that ended Thursday. Ms. Guo described seeing farmers in their 80s or 90s still working in the fields, sometimes with canes or stooped backs. \"They're already very old and don't have the physical ability to work anymore. But the cost of living is high,\" she said, according to the Workers' Daily, a Communist Party publication. The paltriness of the pension is one of the clearest examples of the vast inequalities in China's economy, and the deep-rooted challenges that are often overshadowed by its much-touted progress in hightech fields. While the country leads the world in sectors like robotics or electric vehicles, many of the workers who powered China's economic rise, such as farmers and workers in low-end manufacturing, are suffering from stagnating wages and cutthroat competition.",
      "For many, though, the main argument was more moral than pragmatic. China's farmers had helped build the country, they said, when Mao-era policies required them to sell their crops for artificially low prices, turn their land over to the state and pay agricultural taxes. They acknowledged that urban residents pay more into the pension system than rural residents do, but said the system did not account for the farmers' historic contributions. \"These contributions cannot be measured by simple payment records,\" a commentary in China Business Network, a Shanghai-based media outlet, said. \"Increasing farmers' pensions is not about providing them with welfare, but rather about paying back a debt.\""
    ],
    "paragraphTranslations": [
      "本月，中国政府召开了年度最重要的政治会议，会上提出了宏伟的计划。将继续增加军费开支，致力于在人工智能领域占据主导地位，加强制造业实力。但对于该国一些最弱势的群体，政府的计划则明显没有那么宏大。政府表示，将把农村居民的最低基础养老金每月提高 20 元，即不到 3 美元，每月总额约 24 美元。",
      "这次承诺的涨幅微乎其微，甚至引发了要求增加的广泛呼声—— 连通常在这场年度会议上对政府计划表示赞同的人大代表也不例外。“这对农民来说实在太不公平了，”人大代表、曾被 Mao 赞扬为劳动模范的村干部郭凤莲在为期一周的立法会议上向一位中国记者表示。本次会议于周四闭幕。郭凤莲描述了她所见到的农民：有的八九十岁高龄仍在田里干活，有的还拄着拐杖或弯着腰。“他们年纪已经很大，也没有力气干活了，但生活成本又很高，”据共 C 党刊物《工人日报》报道，她这样说道。\n\n养老金的微薄数额是反映中国经济中巨大不平等现象的最明显例证之一，同时也凸显了尽管中国在高科技领域取得了诸多显著成就，但其深层次的挑战却常常被这些成就所掩盖。尽管中国在机器人或电动汽车等领域处于世界领先地位，但推动中国经济崛起的许多劳动者，比如农民和低端制造业工人，却深受工资停滞和残酷竞争之苦。",
      "然而，对许多人来说，主要的论据更多是道义上的，而非务实的。他们表示，中国农民曾为国家建设作出巨大贡献：在 Mao 时代的政策下，农民必须以人为压低的价格出售农作物，将土地上交国家，并缴纳农业税。虽然他们承认城市居民在养老金体系中的缴纳额高于农村居民，但他们认为，这套制度并没有考虑农民的历史贡献。上海媒体第一财经的一篇评论称：“这些贡献不能通过简单的缴费记录来衡量，给农民涨养老金不是给农民发福利，而是在还账。”"
    ],
