    "byline": "By James Marriott, The Times, PublishedApril 13, 2026",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Temperament is often underestimated as a workplace quality but in public life it often trumps brains and ambition",
      "Of course the asocial genius is now the most overbaked cliché of the tech industry. In the world of elite employment it has long been de rigueur to insist that maverick intelligence matters above every other consideration. Silicon Valley has long had its boring cult of sulky, hoodie-wearing founders and undersocialised coding geniuses.",
      "I have rather more sympathy with the chief executive of the language-learning app Duolingo, who says that when recruiting staff he calls up taxi drivers to find out whether potential candidates behaved well on the way to the interview. I think this is the right emphasis. In all but a few jobs (university physics departments are probably an exception), a good temperament is a much more important workplace attribute than raw brainpower.",
      "How many companies really require their employees to strain to the outer reaches of their cognitive abilities? I suspect most good work, even among the gods of Silicon Valley, has less to do with intellect and more to do with sangfroid, genial spirits, honesty and good judgment. Temperament is arguably the most underrated factor in modern work and public life. It was once prized much more highly. Until the middle of the 20th century most elite universities considered it as much their duty to cultivate \"character\" and \"virtue\" in their students as to churn out irrepressibly ambitious, academic high achievers. The phenomenon of the \"gentleman's third\" described the degree result achieved by the sort of upper-class duffer who was weak on brains but otherwise considered a good egg.",
      "Obviously this culture of gentlemanly underachievement was unsustainable. We should all be glad that it was swept away by the postwar meritocratic revolution of the Forties and Fifties. But it does not follow that merit is all that a society requires to function properly. Politeness, reliability and an ability to keep on an even keel in stressful situations cannot be measured by examination but they remain important traits. More important, I suspect than mere brains. One exemplary product of America's gentlemanly Wasp elite was Franklin D Roosevelt who was famously possessed of \"a first-class temperament but a second-class intellect\". He was also his country's longest-serving and most effective president.",
      "Intellect matters. But I increasingly believe that character matters more. Politicians brooding over how best to prepare Britain's workforce for an uncertain future tend to obsess over \"training\" and \"skills\". We worry that we have too many Mickey Mouse degrees and not enough geniuses studying Stem subjects. These are doubtless valid concerns. But character and culture matter for a society just as much as skills. After all, Britain was guided to its imperial and industrial apogee by classics students. Few in the old elite had what we would now recognise as cutting-edge skills but the country probably benefited from a less tangible culture of optimism, personal agency and social responsibility.",
      "This is obviously not to demand the restoration of chinless good eggs. But it is to wonder whether universities might consider resuming some of their old emphasis on character and temperament. I suspect these qualities are only going to become more important. The advent of Al means that intelligence may soon be the least remarkable human attribute. The antisocial coding prodigies may not have quite the glittering careers they once imagined. What will matter more for human employees will be judgment, honesty and spirit. Or so we can dream..."
    ],
    "paragraphTranslations": [
      "在职场中，性情常被低估，但在公共生活里，它往往比才智和抱负更重要。",
      "当然，孤僻的天才如今已是科技行业最陈腐的俗套。在精英招聘领域，长期以来一直奉行这样一种观念，即特立独行的才智高于一切其他考量。硅谷长期以来一直推崇那种面色阴郁、穿着连帽衫的创始人以及不善交际的编程天才。",
      "我更认同语言学习应用多邻国首席执行官的观点。他说，招聘时会致电出租车司机，了解候选人面试途中的表现。我认为这才是正确的侧重点。除少数岗位（大学物理系或许例外），良好性情在职场中远比纯粹的智力更重要。",
      "有多少公司真的要求员工极限发挥认知能力？我怀疑，即便在硅谷精英中，出色工作更多取决于沉着冷静、性情和善、诚实正直与判断力强，而非智力。\n\n性情可谓现代工作与公共生活中最被低估的因素。它曾备受重视。20 世纪中期前，多数精英大学认为，培养学生的“品格”与“美德”，和培养野心勃勃、学业优异者同等重要。“绅士三等学位”现象，指的是上层社会那些智力平平但品行端正者获得的学位等级。",
      "显然，这种绅士式平庸文化难以为继。战后四五十年代的精英革命将其扫除，我们都应为此庆幸。但这并不意味着，社会只需精英即可良好运转。礼貌、可靠、压力下保持沉稳的能力无法通过考试衡量，却仍是重要品质。我认为，这些品质比单纯的智力更重要。美国绅士派新教精英的典范富兰克林·D·罗斯福，素有“一流性情、二流才智”之称。他也是美国任期最长、最具成效的总统。",
      "智力固然重要，但我愈发相信，品格更重要。政客们为英国劳动力应对不确定未来殚精竭虑，往往只关注“培训”与“技能”。我们担心水学历泛滥，钻研科学、技术、工程、数学的天才不足。这些担忧不无道理，但品格与文化对社会的重要性不亚于技能。毕竟，引领英国走向帝国与工业巅峰的，是古典文学专业的学生。旧精英中，几乎无人具备我们如今认可的尖端技能，但国家受益于无形的乐观文化、个人能动性与社会责任感。",
      "这并非要恢复那些无骨气的好人，而是希望大学重新重视品格与性情。我认为，这些品质只会愈发重要。\n\n人工智能的到来，意味着智力或将很快成为人类最不起眼的特质。孤僻的编程神童，职业生涯或许不再如想象中那般辉煌。对人类员工而言，判断力、诚实与精神品质将更重要。至少我们可以这样期许……"
    ],
