    "folder": "0519英语中，那些你习焉不察的暴力隐喻",
    "titleZh": "英语中，那些你习焉不察的暴力隐喻",
    "title": "Rethinking Violent Language (with Anna Taylor)",
    "topicEn": "International Relations",
    "topicZh": "国际关系",
    "byline": "By Elizabeth Shih, Storytelling Communications, Published January",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A couple of months ago, a meme called \"Evolving from Violent Language\" by Anna Taylor, was posted on Facebook (Meta), and last winter, on Twitter (X).",
      "Taylor is a communications director and \"diversity, equity and inclusion champion\" at the American technology company, Phenomenex.",
      "Leaders of democratic countries which value freedom of speech usually do not want to prescribe what language their citizens \"should\" use, in boardrooms, classrooms, offices, newspapers and other contexts (both on- and off-line).",
      "But in recent times of mis- or dis-information, lies and so-called \"fake news\" (much courtesy of politicians of the right and those inspired by them), we have seen an intensification of violence in thelanguage we use to converse routinely, across many disciplines.",
      "Violent vocabulary has developed from both of the last century's World Wars, and the many other, deadly conflict zones that have transpired since them (e.g. Vietnam, the Middle East, the Balkans, Sudan, Ukraine, etc.)",
      "Consider Taylor's meme, below:\n\nEven these, apparently innocuous, idioms (left-hand column) can evoke violence that is not empathetic, or forward-looking. Over watercoolers and in boardrooms, we tend to inflate in alarmist ways the tone of our speech, which then dwarfs the value of the ideas under discussion.",
      "As a teacher of the English language and as an experienced business communicator, I know that inflammatory use of language can desensitize its readers or listeners, through its exaggerated tone.",
      "The language of the left-hand column (above) presents its ideas as shocking,toxic, excessive and open to garrulity.In so doing, we lose the thinking (logic), discretion, reasonableness and privacy that many of us still value in communication.",
      "Not surprisingly, Anna Taylor's meme has met opposition (including plenty of racist, sexist and ad hominem comments) from others who invest in such \"violent language.\" As humans, we are always already limited by what literary critic and theorist Fredric Jameson called \"The Prison-house of Language.\" But in these chaotic and conflict-filled times, democratic speakers and writers, including Taylor and all of us, still have the autonomy to reflect on, critique and remake some of the \"prison's\" most distorted and confining bars.",
      "Benjamin Franklin once wrote: \"Remember not only to say the right thing in the right place, but, far more difficult still, to leave unsaid the wrong thing at the tempting moment.\"",
      "And now it's your turn: What inflammatory use of language have you observed recently? Do you counter it and how?"
    ],
    "paragraphTranslations": [
      "几个月前，Anna Taylor 做的一张叫“Evolving from Violent Language”的 meme，在 Facebook 上被发了出来。去年冬天，这张图也曾经在 Twitter 上流传过。",
      "Taylor 是美国科技公司 Phenomenex 的传播总监，同时，她也是公司里的“多元、平等与包容理念的倡导者”。",
      "一般来说，重视言论自由的民主国家领导人，通常不会去规定公民在会议室、教室、办公室、报纸，或者线上线下其他场合“应该”使用什么样的语言。",
      "但是，近些年来，随着错误信息和虚假信息的增加，再加上各种谎言，以及所谓的“假新闻”—— 这些在很大程度上来自右翼政客以及受他们影响的人—— 我们日常交流中使用的语言，似乎变得越来越有攻击性、越来越带有暴力色彩。而且这种趋势出现在很多不同的领域里。",
      "这种暴力化的词汇，一部分来自上个世纪的两次世界大战，也来自后来很多致命的冲突地区，比如越南、中东、巴尔干、苏丹、乌克兰等等。\n\n来看一下 Taylor 的这张 meme：",
      "这些左边的表达，看起来好像都很普通、很无害。但它们其实可能唤起一些暴力的画面，而这些画面并不一定是有同理心的，也不一定是面向未来、建设性的。在办公室茶水间的闲聊里，或是在正式会议室里，我们常常会用一种有点夸张、甚至有点危言耸听的方式来说话。结果就是，语言的语气被放大了，反而把真正值得讨论的想法压小了。",
      "作为一名英语教师，也作为一名有经验的商务沟通者，我知道，带有煽动性的语言会通过它夸张的语气，让读者或者听众慢慢变得麻木。",
      "上面左边那一栏的语言，会把本来普通的想法说得好像很震惊、很有毒、很过度，也很容易让讨论变得啰嗦、失控。这样一来，我们在沟通中原本重视的东西，比如思考（逻辑）、谨慎、理性、私密性，就都可能被削弱。",
      "并不意外的是，Anna Taylor 的这张 meme 也遭到了其他惯用此类“暴力语言”者的反对。有些反对意见甚至带有种族主义、性别歧视，或者直接针对她个人的人身攻击。\n\n作为人类，我们本来就已经受到语言的限制。文学批评家和理论家 Fredric Jameson 曾经把这种限制称为“语言的牢笼”。但是，在今天这样一个混乱、充满冲突的时代，民主社会中的说话者和写作者，包括 Taylor，也包括我们每一个人，仍然有自主性。我们仍然可以去反思、批判，并且重新塑造这个“牢笼”里那些最扭曲、最限制人的栏杆。",
      "Benjamin Franklin 曾经写过一句话：“请记住，不只是要在合适的地方说合适的话；更难得的是，在那个最想说错话的（诱人）时刻，能够把错误的话忍住不说。”",
      "现在，轮到你来想一想了：最近你观察到哪些带有煽动性的语言？你会反驳吗？又是如何反驳的？"
    ],
