    "key": "jun0617",
    "date": "2026.06.17",
    "period": "2026年06月",
    "folder": "0617 访朝背后：金同志是如何逆袭的？2",
    "titleZh": "访朝背后：金同志是如何逆袭的？2",
    "title": "How North Korea Won",
    "topicEn": "Politics",
    "topicZh": "政治",
    "byline": "By Jung H. Pak, Foreign Affairs, Published April 21, 2026",
    "paragraphs": [
      "ROCKET MAN",
      "When Kim came to power in 2011 as a fresh-faced 27-year-old, North Korea's moribund economy overwhelmingly depended on foreign aid and trade with China. International aid organizations had long reported that more than 40 percent of North Korea's 26 million citizens suffered from malnutrition, even as the elite inhabitants of Pyongyang enjoyed restaurants and shopping malls. U.S. officials and analysts at the time predicted that the young successor, with no known military or leadership experience, was likely to fail, either quickly usurped by his elders or marginalized as a figurehead.",
      "But Kim came out swinging. He conducted systematic purges to cow the elite into absolute loyalty, and in very public fashion killed his powerful uncle and assassinated his half-brother. He also ramped up the country's nuclear weapons program, conducting over 100 missile tests between 2012 and 2019—more than triple the number his father and grandfather combined had overseen—and four of the country's six nuclear tests, including a hydrogen bomb in September 2017 that had an estimated yield of 100 to 150 kilotons. (For comparison, the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima yielded an estimated 15 kilotons.) By the end of 2017, he had even successfully tested an intercontinental ballistic missile for the first time in North Korea's history. The fall of 2017 was therefore particularly tense. Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump were exchanging insults—Trump called Kim \"Little Rocket Man,\" while Kim called Trump \"mentally deranged\"—and Trump warned that the U.S. military was \"locked and loaded.\" James Mattis, the secretary of defense at the time, pledged \"a massive military response\" to any further North Korean threats, and H. R. McMaster, then the national security adviser, talked about a \"preventive war\" against North Korea. The tension was so thick that even China and Russia supported more stringent UN sanctions on Pyongyang.",
      "Kim, however, is adept at reading the room. In January 2018, he expressed an interest in attending the Winter Olympics in South Korea, providing what Sheila Miyoshi Jager, a scholar of Korean history, has called a \"reassuring pause in a terrifying global game of chicken.\" As the world let out a collective sigh of relief, Kim and Trump stepped back from the brink, and the two leaders engaged in an unprecedented summit in Singapore that June mediated by an eager Moon Jae-in, then South Korea's president. Although Trump declared on social media that there was \"no longer a Nuclear Threat from North Korea,\" the meeting achieved little, and negotiations remained deadlocked in the ensuing months, culminating in a failed second summit in Hanoi in February 2019.",
      "By then, it was clear that North Korea's economy was suffering. South Korea's central bank reported that North Korea's economy had shrunk by four to five percent in 2018. In the negotiations, Kim offered to close part of an aging nuclear research site in exchange for the lifting of sanctions imposed in 2016 and 2017 by the United Nations, which banned North Korean exports of textiles, seafood, coal, iron ore, and labor and limited imports of refined petroleum products. Surmising that Kim would funnel the regained income back into his nuclear program, however, the United States rejected the deal, and Kim returned to Pyongyang with his tail between his legs.",
      "To add injury to insult, a record drought then threatened North Korea's crops, and multiple typhoons battered the country. Thus, the country's citizens were already in an exceedingly vulnerable position when the pandemic hit in January 2020. Trade with China, North Korea's economic lifeline, plummeted more than 80 percent as borders closed, and prognostications for the country grew increasingly grim."
    ],
    "paragraphTranslations": [
      "火箭人",
      "2011 年，27 岁的 JZE 刚刚掌权时，还是一副年轻稚嫩的面孔。彼时，朝鲜经济已近乎停滞，极度依赖外部援助与对华贸易。国际援助组织长期报告显示，朝鲜 2,600 万公民中有超过 40% 遭受营养不良之苦；与此同时，平壤的精英们却享受着餐厅和购物中心带来的便利。当时，美国官员和分析人士预测，这位毫无军事与执政经验的年轻接班人很可能会失败，要么很快被元老夺权，要么被边缘化，沦为有名无实的傀儡。",
      "但 JZE 一上台便强势出击。他开展系统性清洗，胁迫精英阶层绝对效忠，并公开处决了自己权势显赫的叔父，暗杀了同父异母的兄长。他还大力推进朝鲜核武器计划，在 2012 年至 2019 年间进行了 100 多次导弹试验，数量超过其父亲和祖父合计主持次数的三倍。朝鲜迄今 6 次核试验中，有 4 次由他主导，其中包括 2017 年 9 月的一次氢弹试验，据估计其当量为 10 至 15 万吨 TNT。（作为对比，投向广岛的原子弹估计当量为 1.5 万吨 TNT。）到 2017 年底，他甚至成功完成了朝鲜历史上首次洲际弹道导弹试验。\n\n因此，2017 年秋，半岛局势空前紧张。 JZE 与美国总统唐纳德·特朗普相互辱骂。特朗普称 JZE 为“小火箭人”， JZE 则称特朗普“精神错乱”。特朗普还警告称，美国军方已经“弹药上膛、整装待发”。时任美国国防部长詹姆斯·马蒂斯承诺，若朝鲜进一步威胁美国，将予以“大规模军事回应”；时任美国国家安全顾问 H. R. 麦克马斯特也谈到要对朝鲜发动“预防性战争”。局势紧绷到连中俄都支持联合国对平壤实施更严厉的制裁。",
      "但 JZE 深谙审时度势之道。2018 年 1 月，他表示有意参加在韩国举行的冬奥会。朝鲜问题研究学者希拉·三好·雅格称，此举“让这场步步紧逼、险象环生的全球对峙暂时缓和下来”。全世界集体松了一口气， JZE 和特朗普从战争边缘悬崖勒马。同年 6 月，在韩国时任总统文在寅的积极斡旋下，两位领导人在新加坡举行了史无前例的峰会。尽管特朗普在社交媒体上宣称“朝鲜核威胁已经不复存在”，这次会晤几乎没有取得实质成果，随后数月谈判仍陷入僵局，并最终以 2019 年 2 月河内第二次峰会的失败告终。",
      "彼时，朝鲜经济承压已十分明显。韩国中央银行报告称，2018 年朝鲜经济萎缩了 4% 至 5%。在谈判中，JZE 提出关闭一处老旧核研究设施的一部分，以换取解除联合国于 2016 年和 2017 年实施的制裁。这些制裁禁止朝鲜出口纺织品、海产品、煤炭、铁矿石和劳工，并限制其进口精炼石油产品。然而，美国推测 JZE 会把获得的收入重新投入核计划，因此拒绝了这项交易。 JZE 只得灰溜溜地返回平壤。",
      "雪上加霜的是，随后一场创纪录的干旱威胁到朝鲜农作物收成，多场台风又接连重创这个国家。因此，朝鲜民众的处境在 2020 年 1 月疫情来袭前就已岌岌可危。随着边境关闭，作为朝鲜经济命脉的对华贸易骤降 80% 以上，外界对这个国家前景的预判也愈发悲观。"
    ],
