    "folder": "0805中国人为什么喜欢听慢歌？1",
    "titleZh": "中国人为什么喜欢听慢歌？1",
    "title": "Why Chinese People Love Slow Songs · Part 1",
    "topicEn": "Culture",
    "topicZh": "文化",
    "byline": "By Tiffany Ap, Bloomberg, Published July 9, 2026 听课笔记",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Christy Peng was nervous. It was the eighth season of The Voice of China, and she knew her bluesy reworking of a Billie Eilish track leaned on the musical influences she'd picked up while studying in the US. Still, one judge's verdict—\"We're looking for the voice of China, we're not looking for the voice of nightclubs\"—caught her off guard. The remark was edited from the broadcast, but her run on the show ended there.",
      "\"It deeply hurt me,\" says Peng, now a music producer in Beijing. It took a while for the sting to fade, but in hindsight she admits the critique reflected a genuine cultural fault line: Chinese audiences, by and large, value melody over rhythm.",
      "It's a preference that helps explain a striking divergence between China's music market and the West's. While artists such as Adele and Sam Smith have built careers on heartbreak anthems, the slow-burning ballad has largely ceded center stage in the US, UK and elsewhere. Spotify and Billboard charts are now dominated by fast-paced pop, hip-hop and electronic tracks.",
      "In China and the broader Sinosphere, expressive ballads remain the main event, accounting for nine out of the top 10 songs last year on Tencent QQ Music and seven of the top 10 on TikTok's sister app, Douyin.",
      "For record labels, streaming platforms and artists seeking the broadest possible audience in an increasingly globalized music business, understanding this divide matters. Chinese culture's preference for ballads reflects more than musical taste. It offers a window into how hundreds of millions of people think about language, emotion, beauty and human connection—values that help shape not only one of the world's largest entertainment markets but also a distinct cultural worldview.",
      "Alex Taggart, who spent a decade working in China, is no stranger to navigating those differences. Now based in Los Angeles, he runs Isle of Management, helping Western artists enter the Chinese market and Chinese artists expand abroad.",
      "\"A fairly crude rule of thumb is that the Chinese mainstream likes what I would call upper-body music,\" he says, waving his hands from side to side above his head. \"Whereas in the West, it's lower-body music, as in music with a groove\" that you move your hips to.",
      "A computational analysis of 8,562 songs on NetEase Cloud Music, a major Chinese streaming platform, found that from 2011 to 2024, just 4.1% of songs registered as \"passionate and high-spirited\" and 13.7% as \"angry and excited.\" The dominant emotional registers were \"sad and depressed\" at 29.4%, \"calm and relaxed\" at 19.5% and \"confiding and lyrical\" at 18.1%.",
      "That preference extends beyond streaming charts and is noticeable at live events. In May, Hong Kong's Emperor Entertainment Group, a defining force in Cantopop, celebrated its 25th anniversary with a retrospective concert over two nights, tracing the genre's trajectory from the 1980s to the present day. Industry royalty including Joey Yung, Hacken Lee, Hins Cheung and Leo Ku commanded a sea of 40,000 fans each night, who wielded glow sticks and swayed like a neon tide at Kai Tak Stadium. The majority of the setlist never ventured above 90 beats per minute. Instead, the giant arena settled into something like a collective resting heartbeat.",
      "Part of the reason is structural. China has a musical ecosystem built around singing rather than dancing, supported by a 86.3 billion yuan ($12.8 billion) karaoke (KTV) industry. Here, KTV is not a niche pastime but a primary way people consume music, and passing the microphone around requires songs that people can sing. A Diplo track may be a bona fide club banger, but try performing one at karaoke, and the problem quickly becomes obvious."
    ],
    "paragraphTranslations": [
      "彭雅纯（Christy Peng）当时很紧张。彼时正值《中国好声音》第八季，她深知自己对比莉·艾利什（Billie Eilish）歌曲进行的蓝调风格改编，很大程度上依赖于她在美留学期间所受到的音乐熏陶。然而，一位导师的点评——“我们要找的是中国好声音，不是夜店好声音”—— 还是让她措手不及。这句话后来在节目播出时被剪掉了，但她最终也止步于此。",
      "“那句话深深刺痛了我。”如今在北京担任音乐制作人的彭说道。许久之后她才慢慢释怀，但回过头看，她坦言这番点评折射出一个真实的文化分歧：大体而言，中国听众更看重旋律，而非节奏。",
      "这一偏好有助于解释中国音乐市场与西方市场之间一个显著的差异。虽然阿黛尔（Adele）和山姆·史密斯（Sam Smith）等歌手凭借伤感情歌走红，但慢节奏抒情歌曲在美国、英国以及其他地区已经不再占据中心位置。如今，Spotify 和 Billboard 排行榜更多被节奏更快的流行音乐、嘻哈和电子乐占据。",
      "但在中国以及更广泛的华语圈，富有感染力的抒情慢歌依然是主流。去年，腾讯 QQ 音乐榜单前十歌曲中有九首属于这一类型， TikTok 的姐妹应用抖音，榜单前十里也有七首是抒情慢歌。",
      "当下音乐行业全球化程度不断加深，对于唱片公司、流媒体平台以及想要收获海量受众的艺术家而言，弄懂这种音乐差异至关重要。中国文化对于抒情慢歌的偏爱，不仅仅反映了音乐品味。透过这一偏好，我们得以窥见数亿民众如何看待语言、情感、美感与人际联结。这些价值观不仅塑造了全球规模顶尖的文娱市场之一，也造就了独特的文化世界观。",
      "亚历克斯·塔格特（Alex Taggart）曾在中国从业十年，深谙这类文化差异。如今定居洛杉矶的他经营着一家独立音乐艺人管理与咨询公司 Isle of Management，一边助力欧美艺人进军中国市场，一边扶持华语歌手走向海外。",
      "“一个比较粗略的经验法则是，中国主流听众喜欢我所说的‘上半身音乐’。”他说着，抬手在头顶上方左右比划。“而西方更偏向 ‘下半身音乐’，也就是带有律动感的音乐”，让人会随着节拍扭动腰胯。",
      "有人对国内主流音乐平台网易云音乐 8,562 首歌曲做过计算分析，结果显示：2011 至 2024 年间，仅有 4.1% 的歌曲情绪激昂热烈， 13.7% 愤怒亢奋；占据主流情绪基调的分别是伤感低沉（29.4%）、平和舒缓（19.5%）、娓娓倾诉的抒情曲风（18.1%）。",
      "这种倾向不只体现在流媒体榜单上，线下演出同样显而易见。五月，曾深刻影响粤语流行乐的英皇娱乐集团迎来成立 25 周年，连续两晚在启德体育场举办回顾演唱会，梳理粤语流行乐从上世纪 80 年代至今的发展脉络。容祖儿、李克勤、张敬轩、古巨基等乐坛巨星登场时，每晚四万观众挥舞荧光棒，汇成一片流动的霓虹潮汐，全场随旋律轻轻摇摆。整场演唱会绝大多数曲目节拍都不超过每分钟 90 拍，偌大场馆里，观众的心跳仿佛一同归于平缓安宁。",
      "部分原因是结构性的：中国的音乐生态以演唱为核心，而非舞蹈，背后是规模高达 863 亿元人民币（折合 128 亿美元）的 KTV 行业支撑。KTV 并非小众消遣，而是大众接触音乐的主要途径，众人轮流接麦合唱，自然更偏爱人人都能唱的歌曲。一首迪波洛（Diplo）的作品可能是名副其实的夜店热门曲，但如果尝试在 KTV 传唱，弊端立刻显现。"
    ],
